// The *function* form of `push`, with a string value.
//
// `a.push(v)` and `push(a, v)` are the same container write spelled two ways,
// but they reach codegen by completely different routes: the method form is an
// ND_METHOD_CALL and gets the escape rule in `ncg_expr`, while the function
// form is an ND_CALL and gets it in `ncg_call`. A fix applied to one and not
// the other leaves half the language unsound, and nothing else in the probe set
// exercises the function form.
//
// The escape site has to retain, and this probe is shaped to *prove* it rather
// than merely exercise it. A bare escape (`push` then `return`) only exhibits
// the leak half of the bug: with the claim dropped and nothing retained, the
// count stays at 1 and the block is simply never freed — no crash, so a probe
// without the reassignment below passes against the buggy compiler too.
//
// The use-after-free needs the *conditional reassignment*:
//
//     push(outer, s);                                   // escape: claim must be KEPT
//     if (never) { s = producer(); }                    // raises a FRESH claim
//     return outer;                                    // exit block releases s
//
// On the path where the branch does not run, `s` still holds the escaped block.
// If the escape dropped the claim, this assignment re-establishes one over it,
// and the exit block frees a string the array still points at. The read in
// `main`, after `build` has returned, is then a use-after-free.
//
// Verified: fails under ASan when the escape reverts to dropping the claim.
//
// Expected: hello

fn build() -> [string] {
    mut outer: [string] = [""];
    mut s = str_concat("he", "llo");
    push(outer, s);
    if (outer.len > 999) {
        s = str_concat("wor", "ld");
    }
    return outer;
}

fn main() {
    let o = build();
    println(o[1]);
}
